00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:16.860
Few inventions have had as a revolutionary effect on her society as the advent of the steam engine.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.100
This machine allowed the automation of production,

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.700
creating thousands of new jobs in our cities and drawing humans from our farmland existence

00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:25.700
to an ever more metropolitan life.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.540
The population of London doubled in the first half of the 19th century

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:32.700
as people flooded from the countrysides in to London's Kings Cross

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.880
on the very engine that spurred the new trend of urbanization and industrialization.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:40.820
With few options for transport within the city beyond horse and carriage,

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:42.940
these new city dwellers had little choice

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.660
but to live in crowded slums near their places of work.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:49.540
Areas like Whitechapel and Spitalfields that were once home to the prosperous

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:54.100
Victorians and French Huguenots became disease and crime infested homes to the working-class.

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:56.200
These areas had one thing in common.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.620
They were within walking distance of Kings Cross,

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.380
the supply line of the city's new factory workers.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:06.180
London needed a new transport system to allow these workers to spread out and prevent overcrowding.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.220
But with little space above ground for an urban rail system,

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:14.760
London city planners needed a novel approach and so the world's first underground rail system was born.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:21.040
Our ancestors have probably been digging burrows and tunnels since they climbed down from the trees,

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.020
but the earliest tunnels we know of existed as far back as 2000BC.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:28.220
Most tunnels were created for mining or military engineering,

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.940
often through stable rock and over short distances.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:34.240
In fact, the word for "mining" 
and "mine" (used for an explosive device)

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.540
are closely linked for exactly this reason,

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.900
as early tunnel technology was developed in order to approach fortification walls

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.160
undetected to place an explosive charge.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:48.460
One of the primary reasons moats became popular was because ground water mixed with soft porous soil

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.040
was one technical problem these early engineers had no solution for.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.280
Constructing tunnels was and still is dangerous work.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.640
Tunneling accidents were common, whether it be from flooding, tunnel collapse

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.080
due to inadequate reinforcements, or explosions.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.780
The fear of collapse was always on the mind of these workers

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.820
who dug these tunnels with simple tools like pickaxes and shovels

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:10.480
in dark, claustrophobic environments, despite the constant risk.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:15.120
Civil engineering made huge strides in tunnel building techniques over the course of the 19th century.

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:18.100
Starting with the world's first underground railways in London,

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.100
but the development of the London Underground was made vastly easier,

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.640
thanks to the London clay, which the city is built upon.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:28.160
Although the soil today makes it difficult to build skyscrapers, as it cannot support their weight,

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:31.700
it is ideal for tunnelling, being soft yet relatively stable.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.860
London's first underground railway line, the Metropolitan line,

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.040
began in 1863 and was mostly built using the cut and cover technique.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.240
A trench would be dug, walls and a supporting roof structure built, and then buried again.

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:48.620
A safer and easier tunneling technique, but it resulted in the demolition of streets or whole city blocks.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.760
Even today, the remnants of this construction technique can be found

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.420
with hollow buildings hiding the scars of London's past.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.180
The Metro in Paris was largely constructed this way,

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:02.260
and in 1970s Amsterdam, the use of these cut and cover techniques led to the Newmarket riots

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:05.360
and even an attempted bombing at one of the new metro stations.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:08.920
The new 3.5 kilometer line forced a destruction of numerous homes,

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.620
in a time when Amsterdam was struggling with a housing crisis.

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:15.140
The plans for a complete Amsterdam underground were abandoned as a result.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:19.140
No new subway line would be built through the city center until 2003,

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:23.540
with the help of a tunnel boring machine 
which began life in London in 1825.

00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:27.620
Marc Isambard Brunel together with his son, Isambard Kingdom Brunel

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:31.880
invented a technique to make tunneling somewhat safer and less invasive - the tunneling shield.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:36.200
This shield was a scaffold-like wrought iron structure with 36 chambers,

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:37.920
each containing a single workman.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:42.500
These chambers were further broken down by polling boards which were horizontal planks of wood

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:46.560
that the workers removed individually to hew out the tunnel wall to a predetermined depth

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.480
before replacing the board and moving on to the next section.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:54.200
Separating the tunnelling face like this compartmentalized the constant risk of tunnel collapse,

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:58.860
reducing the chance of a small collapse in the face cascading and causing a total collapse,

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.840
burying every worker in the area.

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.280
Once each worker had finished their work, the entire shield would advance

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:07.340
while bricklayers rushed to finish supporting brickwork behind the shield.

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:11.520
This early concept laid the foundation for all future tunnel boring machines,

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:13.260
but much was left to be improved.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:17.440
Construction of Brunel's Thames tunnel was fraught with problems despite this new tool.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:21.860
Water constantly seeped into the tunnel which was a nuisance while pumps were operational,

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:25.040
and a disaster when the pumps failed in the summer of 1826:

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:28.740
resulting in the tunnel flooding up to 12 feet with polluted Thames river water.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:32.220
This, combined with poor ventilation, made working conditions horrendous.

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:34.660
Temperatures rose and plunged with no warning,

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:38.720
and workers complained of illness ranging from headaches and diarrhea to temporary blindness,

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:40.780
and one worker died from his illness.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:43.580
This was just one of many problems that Brunel encountered

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:45.960
and we would need an entire video to document it all,

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:51.480
but the team persevered and 16 years later with the painfully slow progress of just four inches a day,

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:54.400
the tunnel opened to pedestrian traffic in 1843.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:59.080
The project tested the technology of the time and provided vital lessons for future projects.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:04.180
In 1869, a second passage was dug under the Thames from Great Tower Hill to Pickle Herring Stairs.

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:08.320
It was built with a much improved version of the tunnelling shield by Peter William Barlow.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.860
Barlow made one crucial change to the design:

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:14.020
instead of a rectangular cross section, he made the shield circular,

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:18.740
improving its ability to support the surrounding soil and the swelling of the Thames River above it.

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:22.080
This passage under the Thames was fitted with a narrow gauge railway

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.220
and a cable-hauled wooden carriage carried passengers back and forth.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:28.820
This proved to be uneconomic and the company went bankrupt within a year.

00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:32.920
The tunnel was then converted for pedestrian use and became known as the Tower Subway.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:38.100
It was relatively cheap to cross the Thames this way, however, when the toll-free Tower Bridge was opened,

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.240
the subway's income dropped and was sold in 1896.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:44.000
Currently, it's in use as a utility tunnel for water mains.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:47.560
Barlow's design was further improved upon by James Henry Greathead,

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:50.120
when constructing the city and South London railway

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:51.940
nowadays part of the northern line.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:55.760
Using compressed air tunnelling below the ground water table became safer,

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:58.960
as the pressurized air prevented water inflow into the tunnel.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:02.160
Workmen would enter and exit the excavation through an airlock.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.160
This, together with a circular shield and cast iron rings to line the tunnel walls

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:08.280
made for a much safer and efficient job.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:10.800
This is how many of the deep-level tube lines were built

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.520
and travellers on the underground could still see the cast iron rings

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.740
lining the tunnel walls in certain places.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.980
The circular shape of the tunnels this method created inspired the London Underground's name - 'The Tube'.

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:24.820
To this day, most tunnelling shields are loosely based on the Greathead shield.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:28.140
Modern day tunnels like the Royal Oak to Farringdon tube line

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:30.800
are built using TBMs or "tunnel boring machines".

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.320
And these long machines consists of several sections

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:36.600
and essentially form an automated assembly line for tunnel boring.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:40.100
The front section of the machine is a modern version of the tunneling shield,

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:42.740
however, nowadays it features a rotating cutter head.

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:46.760
These rotating heads can be equipped with different tools depending on the type of soil.

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:51.400
As the head rotates, the loosened soil enters the mixing chamber directly behind the cutters,

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:55.280
where a screw conveyor transports the soil out of the cutter head and onto a conveyor belt.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:58.380
This conveyor belt then transports it out of the tunnel

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:01.700
while the machine automatically installs reinforcements as it progresses.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:05.180
These new machines allowed the London Underground to expand even further

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:07.400
with minimal disruption to life on the surface.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:10.840
With possible future improvements decreasing the cost and time needed

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:13.120
through endeavors like Elon Musk's boring company,

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:15.920
our societies could be in for more radical change.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:21.260
With ever-growing city populations empowering people's movement can and will increase our quality of life,

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:27.120
just as these early engineers facilitated a radical societal change for the working class, in cities like London.

